{
  "title": "One Building Holds Up The Modern World",
  "scenes": [
    {
      "image": "videos/10-silicon-shield/1.jpg",
      "seconds": 4.82,
      "text": "Almost every advanced chip on Earth is printed in one place.",
      "delivery": {
        "rate": -4,
        "pitch": -3
      },
      "clips": [
        {
          "src": "videos/10-silicon-shield/v1-1.mp3",
          "atMs": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "image": "videos/10-silicon-shield/2.jpg",
      "seconds": 22.8,
      "text": "The machine that prints the smallest circuits exists in a single version, built by one Dutch company. It fires a laser at molten tin droplets fifty thousand times a second to make light thirteen nanometers wide, and focuses it with mirrors so smooth that scaled to the size of a country, the tallest bump would be a millimeter. Almost all of the leading-edge output runs through Taiwan.",
      "delivery": {
        "rate": 12,
        "pitch": 0
      },
      "clips": [
        {
          "src": "videos/10-silicon-shield/v2-1.mp3",
          "atMs": 0
        },
        {
          "src": "videos/10-silicon-shield/v2-2.mp3",
          "atMs": 5896
        },
        {
          "src": "videos/10-silicon-shield/v2-3.mp3",
          "atMs": 19256
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "image": "videos/10-silicon-shield/3.jpg",
      "seconds": 26.38,
      "text": "Strategists call this the silicon shield. The theory is that being indispensable is its own kind of armor. Nobody wrecks the factory the whole world depends on. But a shield and a target can be the same object viewed from two directions. And the deeper issue is quieter. We built the most complex supply chain in history and then stacked it on a single point of failure, because concentration was cheaper than redundancy.",
      "delivery": {
        "rate": 8,
        "pitch": -1
      },
      "clips": [
        {
          "src": "videos/10-silicon-shield/v3-1.mp3",
          "atMs": 0
        },
